\details{
Logs of zero are undefined, so every log-ratio method, such as
\code{norm = 'clr'}, \code{\link[=aitchison]{aitchison()}}, \code{\link[=alr]{alr()}} and \code{\link[=ilr]{ilr()}}, needs the zeros dealt
with. A \code{pseudocount} added to every value changes the ratios between the
non-zero parts, and acts as an arbitrary weighting. A zero replacement
instead gives each zero a small value and scales the sample's non-zero
parts down to make room for it, keeping their ratios.

The result has no zeros, so it can be given to any of those functions
without a pseudocount (and without the warning that \code{pseudocount = NULL}
gives for zeros). The result is in proportions: every log-ratio method is
scale invariant, so nothing is lost.

\code{mult} gives every zero the same proportion, whatever the sample's depth.
The other methods are for counts: \code{czm} imputes a fraction of one read in
each sample, and the Bayesian-multiplicative methods (\code{gbm}, \code{sq}, \code{bl})
also use how abundant the feature is in the other samples. \code{gbm} is the
common default for count tables.

Conclusions that depend on how zeros were treated are fragile. Repeating
an analysis with each method of \code{list_zero_replacements('id')} is a
simple check.

